window.AILETMOCK={"n":8,"title":"Mock Test 8","theme":"Education, federalism and services","kind":"Full-length paper","blurb":"Education rights, cow slaughter, fair trial, inter-State relations, civil services and backward classes in constitutional law. Seven more branches of law and contemporary legal GK complete the paper.","minutes":120,"neg":0.25,"sec":["Constitutional Law","Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","International Law and Human Rights","Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK"],"q":[{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Education Rights","q":"In P.A. Inamdar v. State of Maharashtra (2005), the seven-judge bench held that the State:","o":["cannot impose its reservation policy on private unaided institutions","can impose it only on institutions that charge fees above a fixed limit","can impose it only on minority educational institutions","can impose its reservation policy on all private unaided institutions"],"a":0,"e":"The Court said that the State cannot take over admissions by regulating them through quotas, and it explained T.M.A. Pai (2002). The 93rd Amendment, adding Article 15(5), later allowed reservation in private unaided institutions other than minority ones."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Education Rights","q":"In Pramati Educational and Cultural Trust v. Union of India (2014), the Constitution Bench upheld Article 15(5) and the application of the RTE Act to:","o":["all schools, including minority institutions of every kind","only government schools and schools that receive aid","private unaided schools, but not to minority institutions","only schools that were established by religious trusts"],"a":2,"e":"The Court held that applying the RTE Act to minority institutions would violate Article 30(1), while Article 15(5) is valid for non-minority private unaided institutions."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Cow Slaughter","q":"In State of Gujarat v. Mirzapur Moti Kureshi Kassab Jamat (2005), the Supreme Court upheld a ban on the slaughter of cows and their progeny, relying on:","o":["Article 25, since cow slaughter is against the Hindu religion","Article 51A(h), which is enforceable like a Fundamental Right","Article 48, as a reasonable restriction under Article 19(1)(g)","Article 14, since the eating of beef is an act of discrimination"],"a":2,"e":"The Court read the Directive Principle in Article 48 along with the Fundamental Duty of compassion for living creatures in Article 51A(g), and held that the ban was a reasonable restriction in public interest."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Fair Trial","q":"In Zahira Habibullah Sheikh v. State of Gujarat (2004), the Best Bakery case, the Supreme Court held that a fair trial means a fair trial for:","o":["the accused, but only at a venue in the State's capital","the accused, the victims and society, so a retrial was ordered","the accused alone, and so it upheld the earlier acquittal in practice","the accused before a jury, since a jury trial is a right"],"a":1,"e":"The Court described the trial court as a 'mute spectator' where witnesses turned hostile, and held that the public prosecutor and the courts must ensure that truth is found."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Federalism","q":"Under Article 249, Parliament may legislate on a State List matter, if the Rajya Sabha declares it necessary in the national interest by a resolution supported by:","o":["two-thirds of the total membership of the House","two-thirds of the members present and voting","three-fourths of the members present and voting","a simple majority of the members present and voting"],"a":1,"e":"The resolution remains in force for up to one year and can be renewed. The law ceases to operate six months after the resolution lapses. Under Article 252, Parliament may legislate for States which consent."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Inter-State Relations","q":"The Inter-State Council under Article 263 is established by:","o":["Parliament, through a mandatory Act made every ten years afresh","the Supreme Court, on a request made by two or more States","the President, when it appears the public interest would be served","the Finance Commission, after the end of each five-year cycle"],"a":2,"e":"The Inter-State Council was set up in 1990 on the recommendation of the Sarkaria Commission. The Prime Minister is its Chairman and the Chief Ministers are members."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Government Contracts","q":"In Bhikraj Jaipuria v. Union of India (1962), the Supreme Court held that a Government contract that does not comply with Article 299(1) is:","o":["void for every purpose, and no remedy of any kind is open to the party","voidable only, at the option of the Government concerned in the matter","fully enforceable, as the formalities are only directory in their nature","not enforceable as a contract, though restitution under Section 70 may lie"],"a":3,"e":"Article 299 requires that a contract be expressed in the name of the President or the Governor and be executed by authorised persons. The requirements are mandatory to protect public funds."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Civil Services","q":"Under Article 310, a member of a civil service of the Union or a State holds office:","o":["until removed by a resolution passed by the State Legislature","for a fixed term of five years, or till the age of retirement","for as long as he enjoys the confidence of the Minister concerned","at the pleasure of the President or Governor, subject to Article 311"],"a":3,"e":"The doctrine of pleasure, which comes from English law, is qualified by Article 311, which requires an inquiry before dismissal or removal, subject to its provisos."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Public Service Commissions","q":"Under Article 317, the Chairman or a member of a Public Service Commission may be removed for misbehaviour by the President only after:","o":["a recommendation by the Prime Minister acting alone","an inquiry by the Central Vigilance Commission","a Supreme Court inquiry that reports he ought to be removed","a vote of no-confidence passed by Parliament in a joint sitting"],"a":2,"e":"The tenure under Article 316(2) is six years or until the age of 65, whichever is earlier. The UPSC Chairman and members are appointed by the President."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Constitutional Amendments","q":"The 104th Constitution Amendment Act, 2019 extended the reservation of seats for SCs and STs in legislatures to 2030 and:","o":["ended the reservation of seats for Scheduled Tribes in legislatures","ended the nomination of Anglo-Indian members to Parliament and Assemblies","introduced reservation of seats for the OBCs in the legislatures","introduced one-third reservation for women in all legislatures"],"a":1,"e":"The amendment affected Articles 330, 332, 334, 331 and 333. The 106th Amendment (2023) provided for women's reservation separately."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Backward Classes","q":"The first Backward Classes Commission, set up under Article 340 in 1953, was headed by:","o":["Kaka Kalelkar","B.P. Mandal","Justice Rohini","Justice Sachar"],"a":0,"e":"The Kalelkar Commission's report was not implemented. The second Commission under B.P. Mandal (1979) recommended 27 per cent reservation for OBCs, which was upheld in Indra Sawhney (1992)."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Official Language","q":"How many languages are listed in the Eighth Schedule of the Constitution at present?","o":["18","22","14","24"],"a":1,"e":"The Schedule originally had 14 languages. Sindhi was added in 1967, Konkani, Manipuri and Nepali in 1992, and Bodo, Dogri, Maithili and Santhali in 2003."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Emergency","q":"Under Article 359, as amended by the 44th Amendment, the enforcement of which Fundamental Rights cannot be suspended during an Emergency?","o":["Articles 19 and 21","Articles 14 and 15","Articles 20 and 21","Articles 22 and 32"],"a":2,"e":"The amendment responded to ADM Jabalpur (1976). Article 19 is automatically suspended under Article 358 only when the Emergency is declared on the ground of war or external aggression."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Local Self-Government","q":"The Twelfth Schedule, added by the 74th Constitution Amendment Act, 1992, lists the functions of:","o":["Cooperative societies, comprising 20 subjects","Municipalities, comprising 18 subjects","Panchayats, comprising 29 subjects","District Planning Committees, comprising 18 subjects"],"a":1,"e":"Part IXA (Articles 243P to 243ZG) deals with Municipalities. The Eleventh Schedule, added by the 73rd Amendment, lists 29 functions of Panchayats."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Seventh Schedule","q":"Which subject was moved from the State List to the Concurrent List by the 42nd Amendment, 1976?","o":["Agriculture","Public health","Education","Police"],"a":2,"e":"Forests, protection of wild animals and birds, weights and measures, and administration of justice were also moved to the Concurrent List."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Union-State Relations","q":"The Sarkaria Commission (1983) examined Union-State relations and recommended, among other things, that:","o":["Article 356 be repealed, and Governors be directly elected","the Rajya Sabha be abolished to strengthen the position of States","an Inter-State Council be set up and Article 356 be used rarely","the residuary powers be transferred from Parliament to the States"],"a":2,"e":"The Commission made about 247 recommendations. The Punchhi Commission (2007) later re-examined the subject, and S.R. Bommai (1994) relied on these ideas to limit Article 356."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Preamble","q":"In Re Berubari Union (1960), the Supreme Court held that the Preamble:","o":["limits the powers conferred on Parliament by every Article in practice","is a part of the Constitution, and is enforceable as law","is a source of power for the legislation of Parliament","is not part of the Constitution, but a key to its makers' minds"],"a":3,"e":"Kesavananda Bharati (1973) overruled this and held that the Preamble is a part of the Constitution. Berubari held that ceding territory requires an amendment under Article 368, leading to the 9th Amendment."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Prime Minister","q":"Under Article 78, it is the duty of the Prime Minister to:","o":["preside over the sittings of the Rajya Sabha each day it meets","communicate to the President the decisions of the Council of Ministers","appoint the Judges of the Supreme Court of India after consultation","advise the Speaker on summoning the Houses of Parliament and sessions"],"a":1,"e":"The Prime Minister must also furnish any information the President asks for relating to administration and legislation, and submit for the Council's consideration any matter on which a Minister has decided without it."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Judiciary","q":"Under Articles 124(2) and 217(1), the retirement ages of Supreme Court judges and High Court judges are, respectively:","o":["60 years and 58 years","65 years and 62 years","62 years and 60 years","65 years and 65 years"],"a":1,"e":"A proposal to raise the retirement age of High Court judges to 65 by the 114th Amendment Bill lapsed."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Prison Rights","q":"In Sheela Barse v. State of Maharashtra (1983), the Supreme Court issued directions to protect:","o":["pavement dwellers facing demolition in Bombay city","tribal communities displaced by large dams in a region in such cases","bonded labourers working in stone quarries of Haryana","women in police lock-ups, who were to be kept in separate lock-ups"],"a":3,"e":"The Court directed, among other things, that female suspects be guarded by female constables and that they be interrogated only in the presence of a relative or a social worker. The cases on quarry workers and pavement dwellers are Bandhua Mukti Morcha and Olga Tellis."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Council of Ministers","q":"Collective responsibility under Article 75(3) means that:","o":["all Ministers go if the Lok Sabha passes a no-confidence motion","Ministers are answerable only to the courts for official acts","each Minister is accountable only to the Prime Minister alone","Ministers may publicly oppose Cabinet decisions on principle"],"a":0,"e":"The Council of Ministers is collectively responsible to the Lok Sabha. Individual responsibility is to the President, who holds office during pleasure under Article 75(2)."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Parliament","q":"Under Article 88, a Minister who is not a member of a House of Parliament may:","o":["vote on money Bills but not speak in that House of Parliament","neither speak nor vote, but may sit in the public gallery alone","vote only if the Speaker has given special permission in writing","speak in and take part in that House's proceedings, but not vote"],"a":3,"e":"The Attorney-General also has the right under Article 88 to speak in either House. A Minister who is not a member of either House must become a member within six months."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Supreme Court Powers","q":"Special leave to appeal under Article 136 of the Constitution is:","o":["available only against orders in criminal cases of death sentence","a matter of right in every civil case above a stated money value","discretionary, and available against any court or tribunal order","available only against judgments of the High Courts in civil cases"],"a":2,"e":"In Pritam Singh v. State (1950), the Court said it would interfere only in exceptional cases with grave injustice. Article 136(2) excludes courts and tribunals relating to the Armed Forces."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"Under Section 66 of the BNS, rape that causes the death of the victim, or leaves her in a persistent vegetative state, is punishable with:","o":["seven years, which may extend to ten years","ten years, which may extend to fourteen years","life imprisonment only, without the option of death","not less than twenty years, up to life or death"],"a":3,"e":"Life imprisonment means imprisonment for the remainder of the person's natural life. The provision replaced Section 376A of the IPC."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"Section 75 of the BNS, dealing with sexual harassment, prescribes for a man who demands sexual favours or makes unwelcome physical contact imprisonment up to:","o":["three years, or fine, or both","seven years, and fine","five years, and fine","one year, or fine, or both"],"a":0,"e":"Making sexually coloured remarks is punishable with up to one year or fine or both. Showing pornography against the will of a woman is punishable like the physical contact offence."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"Assault or criminal force to a woman with intent to disrobe her, under Section 76 of the BNS, is punishable with imprisonment of:","o":["up to one year, or fine, or both","not less than one year, which may extend to three years","not less than three years, extendable to seven years, and fine","not less than five years, which may extend to ten years"],"a":2,"e":"The offence corresponds to Section 354B of the IPC. Section 77 deals with voyeurism."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"Trafficking of a person under Section 143(1) of the BNS is punishable with rigorous imprisonment of:","o":["not less than seven years, extendable to ten years, and fine","up to three years, and fine","not less than ten years, which may extend to life","not less than three years, which may extend to five years"],"a":0,"e":"Trafficking of more than one person carries a minimum of ten years which may extend to life. The offence corresponds to Section 370 of the IPC."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"Under Section 20 of the BNS, nothing is an offence which is done by a child:","o":["under fourteen years of age","under seven years of age","under twelve years of age","under eighteen years of age"],"a":1,"e":"Section 21 covers a child above seven and under twelve who has not attained sufficient maturity. A child under eighteen in conflict with the law is dealt with under the Juvenile Justice Act, 2015."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"Criminal intimidation by threatening to cause death or grievous hurt, under Section 351(3) of the BNS, is punishable with imprisonment up to:","o":["seven years, or fine, or both","five years, and fine","two years, or fine, or both","three years, or fine, or both"],"a":0,"e":"Simple criminal intimidation under Section 351(2) carries up to two years. Anonymous criminal intimidation under Section 351(4) carries up to two years more."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNSS","q":"Under the BNSS, a court summons may be served:","o":["by electronic communication, with the court's seal or digital signature","only by a police constable serving it in person, with no other mode","only by registered post, and never by any electronic means","only by publication in a newspaper circulating in the district"],"a":0,"e":"The provision supports the use of technology to avoid delay in trials. Electronic service on corporate bodies and witnesses is also permitted."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNSS","q":"Under the proviso to Section 223(1) of the BNSS, a Magistrate cannot take cognizance of a complaint of an offence by a public servant in discharge of duty without:","o":["obtaining the sanction of the High Court in every such case","giving the public servant a chance to be heard on the allegation","a prior report from the Central Vigilance Commission or Lokpal","an order by a Sessions Judge directing him to do so beforehand"],"a":1,"e":"The Magistrate must also receive a report from the superior officer. The Section also requires that the accused be heard before cognizance is taken on a complaint."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNSS","q":"Section 396 of the BNSS requires every State Government to prepare, in coordination with the Central Government, a scheme for:","o":["protecting the identity of every witness in cases of sexual offences in practice","giving legal aid to every accused in all criminal cases in that situation","rehabilitating released prisoners in every jail of the State","compensating victims or their dependants who have suffered loss or injury"],"a":3,"e":"The provision corresponds to Section 357A of the CrPC. In Nipun Saxena (2018), the Court issued directions on a model victim compensation scheme for sexual offences."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNSS","q":"Section 397 of the BNSS requires all hospitals, public or private, to provide:","o":["free first-aid or treatment to victims of rape or acid attack","free medical treatment to every person admitted as a prisoner","free treatment to all children below the age of six years","free dental care to all persons held in police custody"],"a":0,"e":"The hospital must immediately inform the police. The provision corresponds to Section 357C of the CrPC and Section 166B of the IPC."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BSA","q":"Under Section 116 of the BSA (Section 112 of the Evidence Act), birth during a valid marriage is conclusive proof of legitimacy, unless it is shown that:","o":["the husband has denied paternity in a written statement to court","the mother alleges that the child was fathered by another man","the child was born within nine months after the date of the marriage","the parties had no access at any time the child could be begotten"],"a":3,"e":"The presumption also covers birth within 280 days after dissolution of marriage, if the mother remains unmarried. In Nandlal Wasudeo Badwaik v. Lata (2014), the Court held that a conclusive DNA result overrides the presumption."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BSA","q":"In Khushal Rao v. State of Bombay (1958), the Supreme Court held that a dying declaration, if found true and voluntary:","o":["is admissible only if the declarant has died within twenty-four hours","can be the sole basis for a conviction, without corroboration","must always be corroborated by other evidence at the trial","is inadmissible unless recorded by a Magistrate in the presence of a doctor"],"a":1,"e":"A dying declaration falls under Section 26(1) of the BSA (Section 32(1) of the Evidence Act). In Laxman v. State of Maharashtra (2002), the Court held that a doctor's certificate of fitness is not mandatory."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BSA","q":"In State of Rajasthan v. Kashi Ram (2006), the Supreme Court held that Section 106 of the Evidence Act (now Section 109 of the BSA), on facts especially within the accused's knowledge:","o":["shifts the entire burden of proof to the accused at the very outset in practice","applies only to offences that are punishable with the death penalty","needs an explanation from the accused, without relieving the prosecution","bars the court from drawing any adverse inference from his silence"],"a":2,"e":"If the accused offers no explanation, or a false one, the court may draw an adverse inference as an additional link in the chain of circumstances. The case involved a wife found dead in a house with her husband."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"Sentencing","q":"In Union of India v. V. Sriharan (2016), the Constitution Bench held that the Supreme Court and High Courts may impose:","o":["only the death sentence, and no other sentence beyond ordinary life","life imprisonment that must always end in release after twenty years","only life imprisonment of exactly fourteen years, with no extension at all","life imprisonment for a fixed term over fourteen years, or for natural life"],"a":3,"e":"The Court upheld the power in Swamy Shraddananda (2008) to impose a modified life term without remission. Remission in such cases is then possible only after the specified term."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Intention to Create Legal Relations","q":"In Balfour v. Balfour (1919), the Court of Appeal held that a husband's promise to pay his wife a monthly allowance while he was abroad was:","o":["enforceable, since her promise to support herself was consideration","enforceable, as it was in writing and signed by both of them","enforceable, but only for a period of six years from its date","not enforceable, as the parties did not intend to create legal relations"],"a":3,"e":"Domestic and social agreements are presumed not to create legal relations. In Merritt v. Merritt (1971), an agreement between separated spouses was held binding."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Offer and Acceptance","q":"In Harvey v. Facey (1893), the Privy Council held that a telegraphed reply stating the lowest price for a property was:","o":["an acceptance of an earlier offer that Harvey had made to buy it","a counter-offer, which Facey accepted by remaining silent","a mere statement of price, and not an offer capable of acceptance","an offer, which Harvey accepted by sending his next telegram"],"a":2,"e":"An invitation to treat or an answer to an enquiry does not make a contract on acceptance. The same distinction explains why goods on shop shelves are generally an invitation to treat."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Offer and Acceptance","q":"Felthouse v. Bindley (1862) is authority for the rule that an offeror cannot:","o":["stipulate that the offeree's silence will be taken as acceptance","make an offer to the public at large by an advertisement in practice","withdraw an offer before it has been accepted by the offeree","specify the mode of acceptance in his own offer"],"a":0,"e":"The Indian Contract Act, 1872 requires acceptance to be absolute under Section 7. Silence can amount to acceptance only where there is a duty to speak or a prior course of dealing."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Privity","q":"In Dunlop Pneumatic Tyre Co. v. Selfridge (1915), the House of Lords affirmed the doctrine of privity, under which:","o":["a beneficiary of a contract can always sue to enforce it","a surety can never sue the principal debtor on the contract","only a party to a contract can sue to enforce it","an assignee can sue only after the contract is fully performed"],"a":2,"e":"Indian law admits exceptions, such as trusts, marriage settlements and family arrangements. Under Section 2(d), consideration may move from any other person, as in Chinnaya v. Ramayya (1882)."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Consideration","q":"Under Section 25(3) of the Contract Act, a written and signed promise to pay a time-barred debt, wholly or in part, is:","o":["void, as it lacks consideration","enforceable even though it is made without consideration","enforceable only if registered under the Registration Act","voidable at the option of the debtor"],"a":1,"e":"The other exceptions to 'no consideration, no contract' in Section 25 are a registered promise made out of natural love and affection, and a promise to compensate for past voluntary services."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Discharge","q":"Under Section 62 of the Contract Act, when the parties to a contract agree to substitute a new contract for it, the original contract:","o":["need not be performed, as it stands discharged by novation","must still be performed, in addition to the new contract","becomes voidable at the option of the promisee","remains in force until a court declares it void"],"a":0,"e":"Novation needs the consent of all the parties. Section 62 also covers rescission and alteration of a contract."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Negligence","q":"In Bolton v. Stone (1951), the House of Lords held that a cricket club was not liable in negligence for a ball hit over a fence because:","o":["the risk to a passer-by was so small that it could be ignored","the passer-by had consented to the risk by walking near the ground","the club had no control over the batsman's striking of the ball","the injury was caused by an act of God and not by a human act"],"a":0,"e":"The standard of care depends on the probability and gravity of the harm. Contrast Paris v. Stepney Borough Council (1951), where the risk was greater for a one-eyed worker."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Negligence","q":"In Vaughan v. Menlove (1837), the court held that the standard of the 'reasonable man' in negligence is:","o":["subjective, as it depends on the defendant's own best judgment","objective, and does not vary with the defendant's own judgment","higher only for a defendant who owns the property in question","lower for a defendant who is of low intelligence"],"a":1,"e":"The defendant's haystack caught fire after he ignored warnings. His plea that he acted to the best of his judgment failed."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Trespass","q":"In the Six Carpenters' Case (1610), the doctrine of 'trespass ab initio' was applied, meaning that a person who lawfully enters property but then commits an abuse:","o":["is liable only for the abuse committed after his entry","is treated as a licensee in respect of the whole entry","is exempt if he has paid compensation for the abuse","is treated as a trespasser from the very start of his entry"],"a":3,"e":"The doctrine applied only to entries authorised by law, such as a distress. The doctrine has been criticised and limited in later cases."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Injunctions","q":"In Dalpat Kumar v. Prahlad Singh (1992), the Supreme Court said that, to grant a temporary injunction under Order 39 of the CPC, the plaintiff must show:","o":["a final decree in his favour, and a bond with two sureties","a prima facie case, balance of convenience and irreparable injury","a prima facie case, proof of fraud, and a pending criminal case","a prima facie case, the defendant's consent, and a registered deed"],"a":1,"e":"The grant of a temporary injunction is a matter of discretion. The Specific Relief Act, 1963 deals with perpetual injunctions in Section 38."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Property","q":"Under Section 54 of the Transfer of Property Act, a sale of tangible immovable property of the value of ₹100 or more can be made only by:","o":["a registered instrument","an oral agreement, followed by handing over possession","a decree of a court, passed upon the buyer's suit","a notarised agreement, along with payment of the full price"],"a":0,"e":"In Suraj Lamp & Industries v. State of Haryana (2012), the Court held that sales through general power of attorney, agreement to sell and will do not convey title. A contract for sale does not itself create an interest in the property."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Trusts","q":"Under Section 5 of the Indian Trusts Act, 1882, a trust of immovable property must be created by:","o":["a signed and registered instrument in writing, or by will","a notarised declaration attested by a Magistrate of the district","delivery of possession to the trustee, without any writing at all","an oral declaration made by the author before two witnesses"],"a":0,"e":"A trust of movable property may be created by a written instrument or by transfer of the property to the trustee. Section 6 sets out the essentials of a trust."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Wills","q":"Under Section 63 of the Indian Succession Act, 1925, a will must be attested by:","o":["a single witness, who is a public servant of the State","two relatives, who are named as legatees in the will","two or more witnesses, each of whom saw the testator sign it","a Magistrate, who is present with the testator at the time"],"a":2,"e":"The testator must sign or affix his mark, and each witness must sign in the presence of the testator. A beneficiary who attests a will loses his bequest under Section 67."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Sociological School","q":"Eugen Ehrlich, a founder of the sociological school, argued that the centre of gravity of legal development lies in:","o":["the judgments of the highest courts of the country","legislation passed by the sovereign body of the State","society itself, and the 'living law' that people observe","the writings of professors of law in the universities"],"a":2,"e":"Ehrlich distinguished 'living law' from the law found in statutes and judgments. His work was an early step in the sociology of law."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Social Contract","q":"In 'The Social Contract', Rousseau argued that legitimate law expresses:","o":["the sum of the private interests of all citizens","the will of the strongest member of society","the command of the sovereign monarch alone","the general will of the community on the facts"],"a":3,"e":"Rousseau distinguished the 'general will', which aims at the common good, from the 'will of all', which is the sum of private wills."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Justice Theory","q":"Aristotle distinguished 'corrective justice' from 'distributive justice' in that corrective justice:","o":["restores equality between the parties after a wrong or a loss","allocates honours and wealth by merit among the citizens","is based on the will of the sovereign alone","is exercised only by the legislature, and not by judges"],"a":0,"e":"Distributive justice concerns the sharing of public goods according to merit. Corrective justice is at the heart of the law of torts and contract."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Economic Analysis of Law","q":"In the economic analysis of law, Richard Posner argued that the common law rules tend to:","o":["promote economic efficiency, by maximising social wealth","follow only the commands of the legislature","protect the weaker party, irrespective of the cost","enforce moral duties, whatever their economic effect"],"a":0,"e":"The school draws on the Coase theorem (1960), under which, if transaction costs are zero, the allocation of resources is efficient whatever the initial assignment of rights."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Precedent","q":"In Quinn v. Leathem (1901), Lord Halsbury stated the principle that a case:","o":["is authority only in the court that decided it","is authority only until a statute is passed on it","is authority for all that the judges said in it","is authority only for what it actually decides"],"a":3,"e":"The principle separates the ratio decidendi from obiter dicta. Obiter remarks are of persuasive value only."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Maxims","q":"The maxim 'delegatus non potest delegare' means that:","o":["a delegate may delegate his power to any person","a delegate must exercise his power only in his own city","a delegate cannot further delegate the power given to him","a delegate is always liable for the acts of his principal"],"a":2,"e":"It is a principle of administrative and agency law. A statutory authority cannot sub-delegate its powers unless the statute permits it."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Maxims","q":"The maxim 'nemo debet bis vexari pro una et eadem causa' means that:","o":["no one should be tried without being heard at all","no one should be vexed twice for the same cause","no one should be a judge in his own cause","no one can be compelled to testify against himself"],"a":1,"e":"It underlies res judicata (Section 11 of the CPC) and the rule against double jeopardy in Article 20(2) and Section 337 of the BNSS (Section 300 of the CrPC)."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Maxims","q":"The maxim 'qui tacet consentire videtur' means that:","o":["he who is silent loses the right to be heard","he who is silent is taken to consent","he who is silent is taken to have denied it","he who speaks first is taken to be the offeror"],"a":1,"e":"In contract law, silence is not acceptance by itself, as in Felthouse v. Bindley, but silence may amount to consent where there is a duty to speak."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Maxims","q":"The maxim 'de minimis non curat lex' means that:","o":["the law punishes even a trifling wrong strictly","the law cares for minors alone, and not for adults","the law concerns itself only with large sums of money","the law does not concern itself with trifles"],"a":3,"e":"Section 33 of the BNS (Section 95 of the IPC) excludes harm so slight that no person of ordinary sense and temper would complain of it."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Maxims","q":"The maxim 'actus curiae neminem gravabit' means that:","o":["an act of the court shall bind every person alike","an act of a party shall prejudice the court","an act of a party shall not be reviewed by the court","an act of the court shall prejudice no one"],"a":3,"e":"It is the basis of restitution under Section 144 of the CPC, where a party who gains by a decree later reversed must restore what he got."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Maxims","q":"The maxim 'lex posterior derogat priori' means that:","o":["an earlier law prevails over a later conflicting law","a general law prevails over a special law in a conflict","a later law repeals an earlier law that conflicts with it","a law of Parliament prevails over a constitutional provision"],"a":2,"e":"It is a rule of implied repeal. It is applied together with the principle that a general law does not repeal a special law, unless the intent is clear."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Interpretation","q":"The principle 'generalia specialibus non derogant' means that:","o":["general provisions do not override special ones on the same subject","special provisions are overridden by a general provision always in practice","a general provision prevails where it is enacted later in time","general words must be limited to things of the same kind"],"a":0,"e":"If a special law and a general law conflict, the special law prevails. The principle is often applied to resolve conflicts between two statutes."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Treaty Law","q":"Article 27 of the Vienna Convention on the Law of Treaties, 1969 provides that a party:","o":["may end a treaty whenever its Parliament passes a repealing law","may suspend a treaty if its Constitution so requires","may refuse to perform a treaty that its courts have held invalid","may not invoke its internal law to excuse non-performance of a treaty"],"a":3,"e":"Article 26 states pacta sunt servanda. Article 46 allows a State to invoke a violation of its internal law only if it was manifest and concerned a rule of fundamental importance."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Recognition","q":"Under the 'declaratory theory' of recognition of States:","o":["a State exists only if recognised by the permanent members of the Security Council","a State comes into existence only on recognition by other States","a State exists once it meets the criteria of statehood, recognised or not","a State exists only after admission to the United Nations"],"a":2,"e":"The Montevideo Convention (1933) lists population, territory, government and capacity to enter into relations. The constitutive theory makes recognition a condition of statehood."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Humanitarian Law","q":"The 'Martens Clause' in international humanitarian law provides that, where a situation is not covered by treaty, civilians and combatants remain protected by:","o":["the orders issued by the commander of the opposing forces","the domestic laws of the State that holds the people captive","the principles of humanity and the dictates of public conscience","the rules that the occupying power prefers to apply in that area"],"a":2,"e":"The clause first appeared in the preamble to the Hague Convention (II) of 1899, and is repeated in Article 1(2) of Additional Protocol I (1977)."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Human Rights Mechanisms","q":"The Universal Periodic Review of the UN Human Rights Council is a mechanism under which:","o":["a panel of independent experts decides on all individual complaints","the International Court of Justice reviews each State's record in turn","only States accused of violations are reviewed by the Security Council","every UN member's rights record is reviewed by other States periodically"],"a":3,"e":"The Review began in 2008. It is a peer review process, with a cycle of about four and a half years."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Human Rights Institutions","q":"The Paris Principles (1993) set out international standards for:","o":["the regulation of private military companies operating abroad","the independence and functioning of national human rights institutions","the protection of refugees in situations of a mass influx","the conduct of armed forces in a non-international armed conflict"],"a":1,"e":"The General Assembly endorsed them in Resolution 48/134. Institutions such as India's NHRC are accredited by GANHRI according to these Principles."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"International Criminal Law","q":"The Nuremberg Principles (1950), formulated by the International Law Commission, provide that acting under superior orders:","o":["is a complete defence for any soldier who is following an order","is a defence only for a senior officer who commands the troops","is a defence only where the order was given in writing form","does not relieve one of responsibility, if a moral choice existed"],"a":3,"e":"This is Principle IV. The principles also provide that individuals are responsible for crimes against peace, war crimes and crimes against humanity."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Territory","q":"In the Island of Palmas case (Netherlands v. United States, 1928), Arbitrator Max Huber held that title to territory depends on:","o":["its geographical proximity to the claimant State's mainland","first discovery of the island, without any further acts","the continuous and peaceful display of State authority over it","recognition of the claim by the League of Nations"],"a":2,"e":"The arbitrator rejected the idea that contiguity gives title, and emphasised effective display of sovereignty."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Custom","q":"In the North Sea Continental Shelf cases (1969), the ICJ held that the equidistance principle:","o":["was a binding customary rule that applied to every State","was a rule of jus cogens, which no treaty could vary","bound Germany, which had ratified the Geneva Convention","was not a rule of customary international law that bound Germany"],"a":3,"e":"Germany had signed but not ratified the 1958 Convention on the Continental Shelf. The Court stated that custom requires settled State practice and opinio juris."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"State Responsibility","q":"In the Factory at Chorzów case (PCIJ, 1928), the Court stated that reparation for an internationally wrongful act must:","o":["wipe out all consequences of the act and restore the prior situation","be limited to a formal apology from the wrongdoing State alone","be a fixed sum that is set by the Security Council in each case in practice","be paid only where the injured State has been actually invaded"],"a":0,"e":"The principle is reflected in Article 31 of the ILC Articles on State Responsibility (2001), which provides for full reparation."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Trade Marks","q":"India's accession to the Madrid Protocol in 2013 allows an applicant to:","o":["obtain a design registration valid worldwide by a single filing","obtain a patent valid in all member countries by a single grant","register a copyright in every member country by a single deposit","seek trade mark protection in many countries by one application"],"a":3,"e":"The system is administered by WIPO, and the Trade Marks (International Registration under the Madrid Protocol) Rules, 2017 give effect to it in India."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Passing Off","q":"The 'classical trinity' of passing off, as stated in Reckitt & Colman v. Borden (Jif Lemon, 1990), consists of:","o":["goodwill, misrepresentation and damage","fame, similarity and intent to defraud","ownership, use and territorial reputation","registration, deception and loss of profit"],"a":0,"e":"Passing off is a common law action, preserved by Section 27(2) of the Trade Marks Act, 1999. A registered mark is not needed to sue."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Trade Secrets","q":"Trade secrets and confidential information in India are protected mainly by:","o":["the Trade Marks Act, 1999, through its chapter on secret marks in practice","contract and equity, as there is no specific statute on trade secrets","the Competition Act, 2002, through its section on secrets","the Patents Act, 1970, through its trade secrets chapter"],"a":1,"e":"A draft National Innovation Bill, 2008 contained provisions on confidential information but it lapsed. Courts grant injunctions for breach of confidence."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Copyright","q":"Under Section 52(1)(a) of the Copyright Act, 1957, fair dealing with a work is permitted for:","o":["only educational use in a recognised institution of learning","any purpose that the author has expressly approved in writing in practice","any commercial purpose, if the user pays a nominal fee for it","private or personal use, research, criticism, review and reporting"],"a":3,"e":"The Indian provision lists specific purposes, unlike the open-ended 'fair use' doctrine in the United States."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Copyright","q":"Under Section 19(5) of the Copyright Act, 1957, if the period of an assignment is not stated, it is deemed to be:","o":["fifty years","five years","ten years","twenty years"],"a":1,"e":"Under Section 19(6), if the territorial extent is not stated, it is presumed to extend within India. The assignment must be in writing and signed by the assignor."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Patents","q":"Under the Patents Act, 1970, a pre-grant opposition under Section 25(1) may be filed by:","o":["only a person interested, after the patent is granted","only the Government, in matters of public health","any person, after publication and before grant","only a competitor, within one year of the grant"],"a":2,"e":"A post-grant opposition under Section 25(2) may be filed by any person interested within one year of publication of the grant."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Plant Variety Law","q":"Under the Protection of Plant Varieties and Farmers' Rights Act, 2001, a farmer is entitled to:","o":["claim ownership of a protected variety on the basis of his own use","register a variety only after paying a royalty to the breeder first","sell branded seed of a registered variety freely in the open market","save, use, sow, exchange or sell farm produce, except branded protected seed"],"a":3,"e":"The Act is a sui generis system under Article 27(3)(b) of TRIPS. It also recognises breeders' rights and community rights."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Competition Law","q":"In Belaire Owners' Association v. DLF Ltd. (CCI, 2011), the Competition Commission held that DLF had:","o":["violated the Consumer Protection Act by delaying possession","abused its dominant position by imposing unfair terms on buyers","engaged in a merger that was not notified to the CCI","entered into a cartel with other builders to fix prices"],"a":1,"e":"The Commission found a violation of Section 4 of the Competition Act, 2002 and imposed a penalty. The case showed that the Act applies to the real estate sector."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Company Law","q":"Under Section 139 of the Companies Act, 2013, an individual auditor of a company can be appointed for not more than:","o":["three terms of five years each in a row","one term of three consecutive years","one term of five consecutive years","two terms of five years each in a row"],"a":2,"e":"An audit firm may be appointed for two terms of five years each, with a cooling-off period of five years afterwards."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Arbitration","q":"In Vidya Drolia v. Durga Trading Corporation (2020), the Supreme Court held that disputes about rights in rem, such as probate or insolvency, are:","o":["arbitrable in every case, if the parties have so agreed","arbitrable only if the High Court has given its prior approval","not arbitrable, whereas rights in personam generally are","arbitrable only where one party to the dispute is the Government"],"a":2,"e":"The Court laid down a four-fold test of non-arbitrability and held that at the referral stage, courts should do only a limited prima facie check of the arbitration agreement."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Data Protection","q":"Under the Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023, a 'child' is a person under eighteen years of age, and a Data Fiduciary must obtain:","o":["an approval from the Data Protection Board in each case","the verifiable consent of the parent or lawful guardian before processing","the child's own written consent, in the presence of a teacher","a certificate from the school at which the child studies"],"a":1,"e":"The Act also bars tracking, behavioural monitoring and targeted advertising directed at children."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Tribunals","q":"Under the National Green Tribunal Act, 2010, the NGT does NOT have jurisdiction over matters arising under:","o":["the Wildlife (Protection) Act, 1972 and the Indian Forest Act, 1927","the Biological Diversity Act, 2002 and the Rules framed under it","the Water (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act, 1974 as amended","the Air (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act, 1981 as amended"],"a":0,"e":"Schedule I lists seven laws, including the Water Act, the Forest (Conservation) Act, the Air Act, the Environment (Protection) Act and the Biological Diversity Act."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Noise Pollution","q":"In In Re: Noise Pollution (2005), the Supreme Court held that the right to a peaceful, noise-free life:","o":["is only a statutory right, and cannot be enforced under Article 21","forms part of Article 21, and the use of loudspeakers may be restricted","does not extend to religious festivals under any circumstance","is available only in residential areas, and nowhere else"],"a":1,"e":"The Court held that Article 19(1)(a) does not include a right to force others to listen. It restricted loudspeakers at night and upheld the Noise Pollution (Regulation and Control) Rules, 2000."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Waste Management","q":"Under the Plastic Waste Management Rules, 2016 (as amended), 'extended producer responsibility' makes:","o":["producers, importers and brand owners responsible for plastic waste they generate","only the State Pollution Control Boards responsible for all recycling work","only local bodies responsible for collecting all the plastic waste of the area","only consumers responsible for the disposal of the plastic they have used in practice"],"a":0,"e":"A ban on identified single-use plastic items came into effect on 1 July 2022. (Verify the current list of banned items.)"},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Sex Workers' Rights","q":"In Budhadev Karmaskar v. State of West Bengal (2022), the Supreme Court directed that, when an adult sex worker is engaged consensually:","o":["her children must always be taken from her by the State","the police must not interfere with her or take criminal action against her","the police must arrest her under the Immoral Traffic (Prevention) Act in practice","her consent is irrelevant for any act of sex work in that situation"],"a":1,"e":"Using Article 142, the Court held that sex workers are entitled to dignity under Article 21, and that the media should not reveal their identity during arrests or raids."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Senior Citizens","q":"Under Section 23 of the Maintenance and Welfare of Parents and Senior Citizens Act, 2007, if a senior citizen transfers property on a condition that the transferee will maintain him and the condition is not met, the transfer is:","o":["deemed made by fraud or coercion, and voidable at his option","void ab initio, even where no such condition was made at all","void, only if the Tribunal registers it within a period of one year","valid, and he can only claim a refund of the value of the property"],"a":0,"e":"In Sudesh Chhikara v. Ramti Devi (2022), the Court held that the condition to provide maintenance may be implied, and that the Act is a beneficial legislation."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Mental Health","q":"Section 115 of the Mental Healthcare Act, 2017 provides that a person who attempts suicide:","o":["to be treated as incapable of giving consent to any act in law","to be punished with simple imprisonment up to one year or fine","to be punished with fine alone and sent for counselling by a court","presumed to be suffering from severe stress, and not punished"],"a":3,"e":"The Act ensures that such a person gets care, treatment and rehabilitation. The BNS does not have a general offence of attempt to commit suicide."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Social Legislation","q":"In Harsh Mander v. Union of India (2018), the Delhi High Court held that the provisions of the Bombay Prevention of Begging Act, 1959, as applied in Delhi, which criminalised begging, were:","o":["valid, but only for begging by able-bodied men","unconstitutional, as they violated Articles 14 and 21","valid, as begging is a nuisance that the State may ban","valid, but only if the beggar is a repeat offender"],"a":1,"e":"The Court held that begging is often the result of poverty and that the State must provide for basic needs. The ruling applied to Delhi only."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Bias","q":"In Gullapalli Nageswara Rao v. A.P. State Road Transport Corporation (1959), the Supreme Court held that a hearing by a Secretary who was also associated with the proposal was:","o":["vitiated by bias, as the same person acted as both advocate and judge","valid, since the Minister had finally approved the scheme","valid, as administrative functions are exempt from natural justice","vitiated only because the hearing was given in writing"],"a":0,"e":"The case concerned the nationalisation of bus routes. The Court held that a quasi-judicial hearing must be impartial, in line with the rule that no one is a judge in his own cause."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Health Law","q":"Under the HIV and AIDS (Prevention and Control) Act, 2017, the HIV status of a person:","o":["can be disclosed only with informed consent or under a court order","can be used by insurers to deny cover in all cases","can be disclosed by any hospital to a person's neighbours","can be demanded by every employer at the time of hiring"],"a":0,"e":"The Act prohibits discrimination in employment, education and healthcare, requires informed consent for testing, and provides for Ombudsmen to hear complaints."},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Governors","q":"In State of Tamil Nadu v. Governor of Tamil Nadu (April 2025), the Supreme Court used Article 142 to:","o":["direct the President to refer the Bills to Parliament for debate","dissolve the State Assembly that had passed the Bills in question","treat ten Bills as deemed assented to, after prolonged delay","remove the Governor from office for disobeying a ruling of the Court"],"a":2,"e":"The Court also laid down timelines for Governors and the President. The Presidential Reference that followed raised the question of the validity of such timelines. (Verify the final position.)"},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Data Protection","q":"The Digital Personal Data Protection Rules, 2025, which operationalise the 2023 Act, were notified by the Government in:","o":["January 2026, after the winter session of Parliament","August 2023, along with the Act itself","March 2024, just before the Lok Sabha elections","November 2025, with a phased implementation in practice"],"a":3,"e":"The Rules provide for phased commencement over about eighteen months. (Verify the dates for each phase.)"},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Labour Law","q":"The four Labour Codes, consolidating 29 central labour laws, were brought into force in India with effect from:","o":["1 January 2023","2 October 2024","21 November 2025","1 April 2021"],"a":2,"e":"The Codes cover wages, industrial relations, social security, and occupational safety, health and working conditions. They recognise gig and platform workers for the first time. (Verify the status of rules in the States.)"},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Tax Law","q":"Under the Income-tax Act, 2025, the twin concepts of 'previous year' and 'assessment year' are replaced by:","o":["a single concept of 'tax year'","a single concept of 'financial year'","a concept of 'accounting period'","a concept of 'income cycle'"],"a":0,"e":"The 2025 Act simplifies the scheme by using one 'tax year' for both earning and taxing income. (Verify the commencement and details against the Act.)"},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Decriminalisation","q":"The Jan Vishwas (Amendment of Provisions) Act, 2023 amended a number of central laws mainly to:","o":["create a new tribunal for the trial of all minor offences","decriminalise minor offences, replacing imprisonment with penalties","transfer the power of prosecution to the State Governments","increase imprisonment for economic offences across all laws in practice"],"a":1,"e":"The Act amended 42 Central Acts to improve the ease of living and doing business. A further Bill was introduced in 2025. (Verify its status.)"},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Arrest","q":"In Mihir Rajesh Shah v. State of Maharashtra (2025), the Supreme Court held that the grounds of arrest must be communicated to the arrested person:","o":["in writing, as a mandatory requirement under Article 22(1)","only orally, by the investigating officer","only if the arrested person asks for them in court","only after the first remand hearing before a Magistrate"],"a":0,"e":"The Court built on Pankaj Bansal (2023) and Prabir Purkayastha (2024). Failure to communicate the grounds makes the arrest illegal. (Verify the details.)"},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Judiciary","q":"Justice B.V. Nagarathna, who is in line to become the first woman Chief Justice of India, is expected to take office in:","o":["May 2028","February 2027","November 2026","September 2027"],"a":3,"e":"Her expected tenure is only about a month. She dissented on the demonetisation judgment (Vivek Narayan Sharma, 2023). (Verify the exact dates.)"},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Legal Services","q":"Under the Legal Services Authorities Act, 1987, an award made by a Lok Adalat on a compromise is:","o":["enforceable only after confirmation by the District Judge","a recommendation only, which either party may reject","deemed a decree of a civil court, final and not appealable","appealable to the High Court within sixty days"],"a":2,"e":"Permanent Lok Adalats for public utility services were added by a 2002 amendment. The scheme gives effect to Article 39A."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Supreme Court Powers","q":"Under Article 139A, the Supreme Court may transfer cases pending before different High Courts to itself where:","o":["the parties are citizens of two different States","they raise the same questions of law of general importance","a case has been pending for more than ten years","the Chief Justice of a High Court has retired from office"],"a":1,"e":"Article 139A was inserted by the 42nd Amendment and modified by the 44th. The Court may also withdraw such cases and itself decide the questions of law."}],"b":[{"id":"S1","m":25,"w":450,"q":"Federalism in India is described as 'quasi-federal'. Examine, with reference to the Sarkaria Commission, S.R. Bommai, Articles 249, 252 and 263, and the Governor's role, how cooperative federalism operates in practice.","o":["Nature of Indian federalism: Union-centric features, Seventh Schedule, residuary powers, integrated judiciary.","Tools of cooperation: Inter-State Council (Art 263), Finance Commission (Art 280), GST Council, Articles 249 and 252.","Case law and reports: S.R. Bommai (federalism as basic feature), Sarkaria and Punchhi Commissions.","Friction points: Governor's role and delay in assent, borrowing limits, Centre's overriding powers.","Conclusion: federalism functions through negotiation and judicial review; strengthening the Inter-State Council would help."]},{"id":"S2","m":25,"w":450,"q":"The four Labour Codes and the recognition of gig workers mark a major shift in Indian labour law. Evaluate whether the Codes balance flexibility for employers with protection of workers.","o":["Background: consolidation of 29 labour laws into four Codes, effective 21 November 2025 (Verify status of State rules).","Key changes: retrenchment thresholds, fixed-term employment, wage floor, social security for gig and platform workers, OSH standards.","Constitutional anchors: Articles 14, 21, 23, 38, 39, 41, 42 and 43.","For: ease of compliance, universal wage floor, wider social security; against: easier exit for employers, limits on strikes, weak enforcement.","Conclusion: success depends on rules, enforcement and tripartite dialogue; test against Article 21 and decent work."]}]};
